WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.981
Here is a small program that uses

00:00:03.062 --> 00:00:04.931
a class called Student.

00:00:05.012 --> 00:00:07.450
It creates a student named Ann

00:00:07.531 --> 00:00:09.724
and prints her description

00:00:09.724 --> 00:00:12.405
then creates Bob and prints his

00:00:12.405 --> 00:00:16.061
and then prints Ann's description once more

00:00:16.061 --> 00:00:18.580
followed by Student.count.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:22.816
The output starts: Ann is student one.

00:00:22.816 --> 00:00:24.862
Bob is student two.

00:00:24.862 --> 00:00:27.930
And then: Ann is student two.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:31.031
Why does the second description

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:33.604
of Ann say two, and not one?

00:00:33.604 --> 00:00:36.000
Pause the video and think.

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:41.675
The answer lies in the three

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:43.771
kinds of variables in Java

00:00:43.771 --> 00:00:46.839
which differ by where they are declared.

00:00:46.839 --> 00:00:50.132
A static variable is declared in the class

00:00:50.132 --> 00:00:51.779
outside every method

00:00:51.779 --> 00:00:53.650
with the keyword static.

00:00:53.725 --> 00:00:55.820
An instance variable is also

00:00:55.895 --> 00:00:59.038
declared in the class, but without static.

00:00:59.113 --> 00:01:01.508
And a local variable is declared

00:01:01.583 --> 00:01:02.780
inside a method.

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:06.010
Where a variable is declared decides

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.330
how many copies of it exist

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:10.170
and how long it lives.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:12.944
Start with local variables.

00:01:12.944 --> 00:01:16.356
In main, x and message are local to main.

00:01:16.437 --> 00:01:18.631
A local variable is created

00:01:18.712 --> 00:01:20.824
when its declaration runs

00:01:20.824 --> 00:01:23.180
and it has no default value

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:26.349
it must be assigned before it is read

00:01:26.349 --> 00:01:29.030
or the program does not compile.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:30.930
Inside the if block

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:33.333
result is local to that block.

00:01:33.333 --> 00:01:36.820
The block prints it: x is greater than 5.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:40.157
When the block ends, result is destroyed.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.780
Its scope runs from its declaration

00:01:42.853 --> 00:01:45.330
to the closing brace of the block.

00:01:45.403 --> 00:01:47.661
So if we remove the two slashes

00:01:47.734 --> 00:01:49.629
in front of the last line

00:01:49.629 --> 00:01:52.907
the compiler stops with: cannot find symbol

00:01:52.907 --> 00:01:53.490
result.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:55.463
A static variable

00:01:55.463 --> 00:01:57.784
also called a class variable

00:01:57.784 --> 00:02:00.260
has exactly one copy per class

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:03.664
however many objects you create, even none.

00:02:03.664 --> 00:02:05.211
It is created once

00:02:05.211 --> 00:02:07.687
when the class is first loaded

00:02:07.687 --> 00:02:10.550
and it lives until the program ends.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:13.732
You use it through the class name

00:02:13.815 --> 00:02:16.564
StaticVariables.course prints

00:02:16.647 --> 00:02:18.563
CSC3100.

00:02:18.563 --> 00:02:22.312
Assigning to it changes the one shared copy

00:02:22.312 --> 00:02:25.477
and inside the class, the short name

00:02:25.477 --> 00:02:27.059
course, works too.

00:02:27.059 --> 00:02:28.642
Outside the class

00:02:28.642 --> 00:02:31.641
always write the class name, a dot

00:02:31.641 --> 00:02:33.140
and the variable.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.155
An instance variable, or field

00:02:36.155 --> 00:02:39.376
is declared in the class without static

00:02:39.454 --> 00:02:41.732
and every object gets its own

00:02:41.811 --> 00:02:43.775
copy when new creates it.

00:02:43.775 --> 00:02:46.681
A field starts with a default value

00:02:46.681 --> 00:02:49.666
zero for numbers, false for booleans

00:02:49.666 --> 00:02:51.630
and null for references.

00:02:51.708 --> 00:02:53.672
Then the constructor runs

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:55.400
and sets name to Ada.

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:59.158
The variable obj refers to the object

00:02:59.158 --> 00:03:02.297
and a field is reached through it.

00:03:02.297 --> 00:03:04.181
obj.name is Ada

00:03:04.181 --> 00:03:06.782
and obj.i was never set

00:03:06.782 --> 00:03:10.190
so it prints its default value, zero.

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:12.733
Now the Student class.

00:03:12.733 --> 00:03:14.249
count is static

00:03:14.249 --> 00:03:16.925
one copy for the whole class.

00:03:16.925 --> 00:03:19.601
name is an instance variable

00:03:19.601 --> 00:03:22.010
one copy in every student.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.908
The constructor stores the name

00:03:24.908 --> 00:03:27.853
writing this.name for the field

00:03:27.853 --> 00:03:30.560
and adds one to the shared count.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:34.387
The method describe builds a local string

00:03:34.387 --> 00:03:36.207
temp, and returns it.

00:03:36.207 --> 00:03:39.270
When describe returns, temp is gone.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:41.994
Let's run it on the machine.

00:03:41.994 --> 00:03:43.985
When the class is loaded

00:03:43.985 --> 00:03:47.354
count is created once, with the value zero.

00:03:47.354 --> 00:03:50.570
It lives in the class, not in any object.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:53.051
new Student("Ann") creates an

00:03:53.133 --> 00:03:55.183
object with its own name

00:03:55.265 --> 00:03:57.396
and the constructor raises

00:03:57.478 --> 00:03:59.445
the shared count to one.

00:03:59.445 --> 00:04:02.560
So describe says: Ann is student one.

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:06.560
new Student("Bob") creates a second object

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:08.386
with a second name

00:04:08.386 --> 00:04:11.580
but there is still only one count

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:13.405
and it becomes two.

00:04:13.405 --> 00:04:15.230
Bob is student two.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:17.918
Now a.describe() runs again.

00:04:17.918 --> 00:04:19.710
Its name is still Ann

00:04:19.788 --> 00:04:21.892
but it reads the same count

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:23.684
as everyone else: 2.

00:04:23.684 --> 00:04:25.632
That answers the puzzle

00:04:25.632 --> 00:04:28.359
count is not stored in the object

00:04:28.359 --> 00:04:30.307
it belongs to the class.

00:04:30.307 --> 00:04:33.580
And at the end, Student.count is 2.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:36.266
Here is the whole comparison.

00:04:36.338 --> 00:04:38.153
A local variable lives in

00:04:38.226 --> 00:04:41.057
a method or a block: one copy per call

00:04:41.057 --> 00:04:44.252
until the block ends, and no default value.

00:04:44.324 --> 00:04:46.793
A static variable has one copy per

00:04:46.865 --> 00:04:48.898
class for the whole program

00:04:48.898 --> 00:04:51.003
used as class name.name.

00:04:51.076 --> 00:04:52.818
An instance variable has

00:04:52.891 --> 00:04:54.343
one copy per object

00:04:54.343 --> 00:04:56.448
lives as long as the object

00:04:56.448 --> 00:04:58.772
and is used as object.name.

00:04:58.772 --> 00:05:01.240
Both of these get default values.

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:03.287
Now the statements.

00:05:03.365 --> 00:05:05.681
An if statement runs its block

00:05:05.759 --> 00:05:07.844
when the condition is true

00:05:07.844 --> 00:05:10.855
and the optional else block otherwise.

00:05:10.855 --> 00:05:14.176
The condition must be a boolean; unlike C

00:05:14.176 --> 00:05:16.416
Java does not accept an int.

00:05:16.416 --> 00:05:18.655
Here i is 10, so it prints

00:05:18.655 --> 00:05:20.740
i is smaller than 15.

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:23.776
Be careful about what else means.

00:05:23.857 --> 00:05:26.787
The else of i < 15 is

00:05:26.869 --> 00:05:29.555
i >= 15:

00:05:29.555 --> 00:05:32.810
it also runs when i is exactly 15.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:35.914
When equality matters, use else if.

00:05:35.914 --> 00:05:37.723
Here score is 15.

00:05:37.723 --> 00:05:40.241
It is not greater than 15

00:05:40.241 --> 00:05:41.579
but it is equal

00:05:41.579 --> 00:05:43.860
so the second branch prints

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:46.142
15 is equal to 15.

00:05:46.142 --> 00:05:49.053
And always use braces: without them

00:05:49.053 --> 00:05:52.200
only one statement belongs to a branch.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:56.256
Relational operators such as <

00:05:56.362 --> 00:05:59.944
and == compare two

00:06:00.049 --> 00:06:02.788
values and give a boolean.

00:06:02.788 --> 00:06:07.528
The logical operators combine booleans: and

00:06:07.528 --> 00:06:08.898
or, and not.

00:06:08.898 --> 00:06:13.428
With x true and y false, x and y is false

00:06:13.428 --> 00:06:17.220
x or y is true, and not x is false.

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:18.692
Now s is null.

00:06:18.692 --> 00:06:21.498
The condition asks: is s not null

00:06:21.498 --> 00:06:24.625
and is s.length > 0?

00:06:24.625 --> 00:06:27.191
The left side is already false

00:06:27.191 --> 00:06:30.397
so Java never evaluates the right side.

00:06:30.397 --> 00:06:33.925
which would throw a null pointer exception.

00:06:33.925 --> 00:06:36.410
It prints: s is null or empty.

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:40.526
This is called short-circuit evaluation

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:42.786
and data-structure code

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:44.857
uses it all the time

00:06:44.857 --> 00:06:47.117
while node is not null

00:06:47.117 --> 00:06:50.600
and node.value is less than key.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:53.369
A while loop tests a condition

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:55.418
and runs its body as long as

00:06:55.488 --> 00:06:57.042
the condition is true.

00:06:57.042 --> 00:06:58.808
This one adds up e: 1

00:06:58.808 --> 00:07:00.856
plus 1 over 1 factorial

00:07:00.856 --> 00:07:03.681
plus 1 over 2 factorial, and so on

00:07:03.751 --> 00:07:06.718
and it stops when the next term is smaller

00:07:06.788 --> 00:07:09.260
than 0.0001.

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:10.473
Trace it.

00:07:10.473 --> 00:07:13.926
At first, term is 1, sum is 0

00:07:13.926 --> 00:07:15.268
and k is 1.

00:07:15.268 --> 00:07:18.337
The first pass adds 1 to sum

00:07:18.337 --> 00:07:22.173
divides term by 1, and k becomes 2.

00:07:22.173 --> 00:07:24.954
The second pass: sum is 2

00:07:24.954 --> 00:07:28.694
term becomes 0.5, and k is 3.

00:07:28.694 --> 00:07:31.380
Then sum is 2.5

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:35.599
term is about 0.1667

00:07:35.695 --> 00:07:38.093
and then sum is about

00:07:38.188 --> 00:07:40.490
2.6667.

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:42.971
Each pass makes term smaller

00:07:42.971 --> 00:07:45.984
so the loop must end: on the ninth test

00:07:45.984 --> 00:07:48.482
term is 1 over 8 factorial

00:07:48.482 --> 00:07:51.201
below the limit, and the loop stops.

00:07:51.201 --> 00:07:52.964
Eight terms were added

00:07:53.038 --> 00:07:55.316
and the result agrees with Math.E

00:07:55.389 --> 00:07:57.520
to four decimal places.

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:00.308
If you forgot to divide term by k

00:08:00.308 --> 00:08:03.104
the condition would stay true forever.

00:08:03.104 --> 00:08:04.897
Before you write a loop

00:08:04.968 --> 00:08:07.549
name the variable that moves towards

00:08:07.621 --> 00:08:09.270
the stopping condition.

00:08:09.850 --> 00:08:12.351
A for loop puts the three parts of

00:08:12.424 --> 00:08:14.483
a counting loop on one line.

00:08:14.483 --> 00:08:16.690
The initialisation runs once.

00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:19.631
Then, as long as the condition is true

00:08:19.631 --> 00:08:22.500
the body runs, followed by the update.

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:24.485
This body adds 1 over i

00:08:24.485 --> 00:08:26.618
for i from 1 to 100

00:08:26.692 --> 00:08:28.751
and the program prints about

00:08:28.824 --> 00:08:30.810
5.187.

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:34.425
Notice the decimal point in the numerator.

00:08:34.425 --> 00:08:37.924
If the body were sum += 1 / i

00:08:37.924 --> 00:08:39.789
with both operands int

00:08:39.789 --> 00:08:42.666
the / would be integer division

00:08:42.666 --> 00:08:44.687
which drops the fraction

00:08:44.687 --> 00:08:47.719
1 over 1 is 1, but 1 over 2

00:08:47.719 --> 00:08:50.984
1 over 3 and all the rest are 0.

00:08:51.062 --> 00:08:52.850
The program would print

00:08:52.928 --> 00:08:54.560
a sum of exactly 1.

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:57.637
Two more keywords control a loop.

00:08:57.637 --> 00:08:59.982
break leaves the loop at once.

00:08:59.982 --> 00:09:02.781
continue skips the rest of the body

00:09:02.781 --> 00:09:05.580
and goes on with the next iteration.

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:06.741
Trace it.

00:09:06.741 --> 00:09:08.993
1 and 2 are printed.

00:09:08.993 --> 00:09:11.695
3 is a multiple of 3

00:09:11.695 --> 00:09:13.676
so continue skips it.

00:09:13.676 --> 00:09:17.549
4 and 5 are printed, 6 is skipped

00:09:17.549 --> 00:09:19.531
and 7 is printed.

00:09:19.531 --> 00:09:22.683
At 8, i is greater than 7

00:09:22.683 --> 00:09:24.845
so break ends the loop

00:09:24.845 --> 00:09:27.367
and 9 and 10 never run.

00:09:27.367 --> 00:09:31.330
The output is: 1, 2, 4, 5, 7.

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:32.691
To sum up.

00:09:32.691 --> 00:09:35.425
1: a local variable lives inside

00:09:35.503 --> 00:09:38.784
its method or block, has no default value

00:09:38.784 --> 00:09:41.362
and is gone when the block ends.

00:09:41.362 --> 00:09:44.174
2: a static variable has 1 copy

00:09:44.252 --> 00:09:45.814
for the whole class

00:09:45.892 --> 00:09:48.079
and an instance variable has

00:09:48.157 --> 00:09:50.110
1 copy in every object.

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:50.657
3

00:09:50.735 --> 00:09:53.703
every loop needs a variable that moves

00:09:53.781 --> 00:09:56.203
towards its stopping condition

00:09:56.281 --> 00:09:58.155
and an int divided by an

00:09:58.234 --> 00:10:00.030
int drops the fraction.

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:01.798
Try this yourself.

00:10:01.877 --> 00:10:04.240
Exercise 1 in the notes is a

00:10:04.319 --> 00:10:06.603
short program called Average

00:10:06.603 --> 00:10:08.021
with 4 errors.

00:10:08.021 --> 00:10:10.542
Two of them are today's topics:

00:10:10.542 --> 00:10:14.086
a local variable read before it is assigned

00:10:14.086 --> 00:10:17.159
and a loop that runs 1 step too far.

00:10:17.159 --> 00:10:18.340
Find all 4.

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:22.194
In part 3, we write our own classes,

00:10:22.194 --> 00:10:25.610
create objects, and handle exceptions.

